## In 30 seconds

Icon font bridges expose type-safe enums implementing **IconFont**. Call `FontAwesome.home.use(button, 18)` — font and glyph text are applied in one step. Each font is a separate Maven artifact under `org.jrebirth.af.iconfont-bridge`.

Quick Start
---------------------

Add an icon-font bridge artifact and call `use()` on any JavaFX `Labeled` control.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af.iconfont-bridge</groupId>
    <artifactId>fontawesome</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

**Minimal example — icon on a Button in a View:**

```java
@Override
protected void initView() {
    Button saveBtn = new Button();
    FontAwesome.floppy_o.use(saveBtn, 18.0);
    node().getChildren().add(saveBtn);
}
```

Each enum constant implements `IconFont` and maps to a unicode glyph bundled with the bridge module. No magic strings or manual font loading required.


IconFont
==============


Overview
---------------------

JRebirth provides **bridge modules** for popular icon fonts, allowing type-safe icon usage in JavaFX applications. Instead of referencing icons by magic strings or unicode code points scattered throughout your code, you use strongly-typed enum constants that are checked at compile time.

Each icon font is packaged as a separate Maven artifact under `org.jrebirth.af.iconfont-bridge`, making it easy to include only the fonts you need.


Available Icon Font Bridges
---------------------

| Artifact | Enum Class | Icons |
|----------|-----------|-------|
| `fontawesome` | `FontAwesome` | 600+ general-purpose icons |
| `emojione` | `EmojiOne` | Emoji icons |
| `icons525` | `Icons525` | 525 UI icons |
| `typicons` | `Typicons` | Typicons icon set |
| `weathericons` | `WeatherIcons` | Weather-themed icons |


IconFont Interface
---------------------

Every icon font enum implements the **IconFont** interface from `org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided`. This interface provides:

* `charCode()` &mdash; returns the unicode character representing the icon.
* `item()` &mdash; returns the `FontItem` resource for loading the font at a given size.
* `use(Labeled l)` &mdash; applies the icon to any JavaFX `Labeled` control (Button, Label, etc.).
* `use(Labeled l, double fontSize)` &mdash; applies the icon with a custom font size.
* `name()` &mdash; returns the enum constant name.

The `use()` methods set both the font and the text content in one call, making icon application a single-line operation.


```java
public enum FontAwesome implements IconFont {

    glass("\uf000"),
    music("\uf001"),
    search("\uf002"),
    heart("\uf004"),
    home("\uf015"),
    check("\uf00c"),
    remove("\uf00d"), close("\uf00d"), times("\uf00d"),
    gear("\uf013"), cog("\uf013");

    private final String charCode;

    private FontAwesome(final String charCode) {
        this.charCode = charCode;
    }

    @Override
    public String charCode() {
        return this.charCode;
    }

    @Override
    public FontItem item() {
        return ITEM;
    }
}
```

Each constant stores its unicode escape. Alias names (`close`, `times`, `cog`) share glyphs. Call `FontAwesome.search.use(button)` to apply font and text in one step.


Usage Pattern
---------------------

### Adding a Dependency ###

Add the desired icon font bridge to your `pom.xml`:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af.iconfont-bridge</groupId>
    <artifactId>fontawesome</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

### Applying an Icon ###

Reference the enum constant and call `use()` on any `Labeled` node:

```java
Label iconLabel = new Label();
FontAwesome.home.use(iconLabel);

Button searchBtn = new Button();
FontAwesome.search.use(searchBtn, 24.0);
```

### Using in a View ###

In a JRebirth View, icons are typically applied during `initView()`:

```java
@Override
protected void initView() {
    Label navIcon = new Label();
    FontAwesome.bars.use(navIcon);
    node().getChildren().add(navIcon);
}
```


FontIcon Showcase
---------------------

The JRebirth showcase includes a **FontIcon** module that displays all available icons from each font in a tabbed, scrollable browser. It demonstrates how to use `IconFont` enums with `TabbedPaneModel` for an interactive catalog.

### FontIconView ###

The showcase view creates a tile grid for each icon in a font, displaying both the icon glyph and its name:


```java
public class FontIconView extends DefaultView<FontIconModel, ScrollPane, NullController> {

    public FontIconView(final FontIconModel model) {
        super(model);
    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initView() {

        node().setFitToWidth(true);
        node().setFitToHeight(true);

        final FlowPane pane = new FlowPane(Orientation.HORIZONTAL);
        pane.setPadding(new Insets(10));
        pane.setHgap(4.0);
        pane.setVgap(4.0);

        pane.setAlignment(Pos.CENTER);

        pane.maxWidthProperty().bind(((Region) node().getParent()).widthProperty());

        final Class<? extends IconFont> c = model().object();
        Stream.of(c.getEnumConstants()).filter(c::isInstance).map(c::cast).forEach(iconFont -> pane.getChildren().add(buildTile(iconFont)));

        // pane.setStyle("-fx-background-color:blue;");
        node().setContent(pane);
    }

    private Node buildTile(final IconFont iconFont) {
        final VBox box = new VBox();
        box.setAlignment(Pos.CENTER);
        box.setPrefSize(100, 50);

        final Label icon = new Label();
        iconFont.use(icon);
        icon.setPadding(new Insets(4));
        icon.setAlignment(Pos.CENTER);
        VBox.setVgrow(icon, Priority.ALWAYS);

        final Label text = new Label(iconFont.name());
        text.setAlignment(Pos.BOTTOM_CENTER);
        VBox.setVgrow(text, Priority.SOMETIMES);

        text.setTooltip(new Tooltip(iconFont.name()));

        box.getChildren().addAll(icon, text);

        // box.setStyle("-fx-background-color:red;");
        return box;
    }

}
```

### MainModel ###

The showcase main model assembles tabs for each available icon font (FontAwesome, EmojiOne, Icons525, WeatherIcons, Typicons):


```java
@Register(value = ModuleModel.class)
public class MainModel extends DefaultSimpleModel<BorderPane> implements ModuleModel {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initModel() {

    }

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initSimpleView() {

    	final FadeInOutWaveBean fiowb = FadeInOutWaveBean.create().showDuration(Duration.millis(4000));

        final Stream<Dockable> tabs = Stream.of(FontAwesome.class, EmojiOne.class, Icons525.class, WeatherIcons.class, Typicons.class)
                                            .map(c -> Dockable.create()
                                                              .name(c.getSimpleName())
                                                              .modelKey(Key.createMulti(FontIconModel.class, c)));

        final TabbedPaneConfig cfg = TabbedPaneConfig.create()
                                                     .id("FontIcon")
                                                     .tabs(tabs.toArray(Dockable[]::new));

        attachUi(TabbedPaneModel.class, WBuilder.buildUiData(node().centerProperty(), fiowb, Collections.singletonList(cfg)).toArray(new WaveData[0]));
    }

    @Override
    public String moduleName() {
        return "FontIcon";
    }

}
```


JPMS Module Configuration
---------------------

When using JPMS (Java Platform Module System), ensure your `module-info.java` opens or requires the icon font bridge module:

```java
requires org.jrebirth.af.iconfontbridge.fontawesome;
```

The font files are bundled as resources within each bridge module and are loaded automatically via the JRebirth resource system.


